  "num_turns": null,
  "final_message": "I fixed the bug in `session_ttl.py`, and `python3 -m unittest -v test_public.py` now passes.\n\n**The bug:** on line 10, `now` was parsed and then had its timezone removed with `.replace(tzinfo=None)`, while `created` kept its timezone. Subtracting a timestamp without a timezone from one with a timezone raises a `TypeError`. That's the error you got for inputs ending in `+00:00`.\n\n**The fix:** `now` is now parsed with `datetime.fromisoformat(now_iso)` and nothing else, the same way `created` is. The subtraction now takes the timezone offsets into account.\n\nI also checked by hand:\n- 30 seconds elapsed with a 60-second TTL returns `False`.\n- `00:00:00+00:00` to `01:00:30+01:00` is 30 seconds in real time, so it returns `False` and the offsets are handled correctly.\n- Two timestamps without a timezone, 60 seconds apart, return `True`.\n\nTwo things I left alone:\n- **Mixed inputs:** if one timestamp has a timezone and the other doesn't, the function still raises `TypeError`. The README only covers timezone-aware inputs, so I didn't add a rule for that case.\n- **Formatting:** the formatter flags the file because of the long line 7, which was already there before my change. I left it as is so the change stays one line and `format_expiry_message` isn't touched.\n\nDONE: Stopped removing the timezone from `now_iso` so both timestamps are compared with their offsets, which fixes the naive/aware TypeError; the public test passes.",
