      "final_message": "I rewrote `solution.py` so `merge_intervals` follows the README. Both public tests pass under `python3 -m unittest -v test_public.py`.\n\n- **Input checks:** passing something other than a list raises `TypeError`. Any interval that isn't a two-element list/tuple of plain ints (bools are rejected), or that has `start > end`, raises `ValueError`.\n- **Merging:** intervals are sorted by start. The next interval is merged into the current one when it begins at or before the current end, so touching intervals like `[1,2],[2,3]` become `[1,3]`.\n- **Output:** always a new list of `[start, end]` lists, and the input is never changed. An empty input returns `[]`.\n\nI also ran some quick ad-hoc checks beyond the public tests: tuple inputs, the input staying unchanged, empty input, touching intervals, a bool value, a wrong-length interval, a float value, and a non-list input. All behaved as the README describes.\n\nDONE: Rewrote merge_intervals to validate input (TypeError/ValueError), sort, and merge overlapping or touching intervals into a new list without changing the input; public tests pass.",
